  "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 6

Iterations 4 and 5 both ended on the same word. Four wrote *the ranked next
candidate is now allocation rather than any peephole*; five, arriving from the
other ISA, wrote *Allocator. Iteration 04 already has the workload.* This is
that.

## What was actually there

The pool was four registers per target, and running out was a cliff rather
than a slope: `allocate-without-spills` fails whole, and `allocate-with-spills`
then gives every value its own stack slot. Measured by handing the allocator
dummy registers and counting frame slots:

| pool | kernel_wide frame slots |
|---:|---:|
| 4 through 9 | 166 |
| 10 and up | 0 |

**One register was the difference between all of memory and none of it**, and
it read the same on both ISAs.

## The change

Both ABIs make more registers available than four. Two tiers now sit behind
the original scratch tier:

- **leaf** — caller-saved, offered only to functions that call nothing:
  x86-64's incoming argument registers, dead once the entry plan has copied
  the arguments out, and AArch64's temporaries below the encoder's scratch
  pair. Free; nothing is saved.
- **preserved** — callee-saved, available everywhere, at the cost of a save
  and a restore for exactly the registers a body names.

The decision that mattered was **not** how wide to make the pool. It was to
hand it out **one register at a time, and only once the free list is empty and
there is no dying operand to reuse.**

The first attempt offered the whole pool up front. It was correct and it was
worse: the free list is a queue, so allocation round-robins, and a body needing
three registers touched all nineteen — reaching the preserved tier and paying
saves for registers it never needed. It also moved every value in every small
body, which turned 43 byte-exact tests red at once. Growing on demand leaves
what already fit exactly where it was: **the benchmark kernel that fits
compiles to byte-identical output on both ISAs.**

## Measure

21 rotated samples per pair, Apple M4, each pair built from the same amu and
differing only in the allocator. Every variant answered 8896821931.

| workload | ISA | before | after | |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| kernel | AArch64 | 272 B | 272 B | byte-identical |
| kernel | x86-64 | 474 B | 474 B | byte-identical |
| **kernel_wide** | **AArch64** | **1940 B / 41.4 ns** | **612 B / 10.0 ns** | **4.15x** |
| **kernel_wide** | **x86-64 (Rosetta)** | **3793 B / 78.2 ns** | **1044 B / 25.5 ns** | **3.07x** |

Against LLVM on the same rotation, AArch64 native: `kernel_wide` is **1.62x**
where iteration 4 measured **9.10x**. The eight-lane kernel now runs at roughly
the speed of the one-lane kernel while doing eight times the arithmetic.

## The check that had to be built

A frame that saves a callee-saved register and forgets to restore it is silent
where the defect is: the caller keeps running with a corrupted register and the
wrong number surfaces somewhere else. The first harness written for it reported
`preserved` against deliberately broken code — because AArch64's `kernel_wide`
never reaches the preserved tier at all (its leaf tier is seven wide), so
there was nothing to clobber and nothing to notice.

What discriminates: x86-64 does reach it. Restoring into the wrong register,
dropping the AArch64 restore, and restoring the pairs in the wrong order each
turn exactly one test red, and the runtime harness names precisely the four
registers the compiler said it saved.

**The two ISAs reach the preserved tier at pressures that do not overlap** —
seven to eleven live values on x86-64, twelve to nineteen on AArch64. Below the
window the leaf tier still has room; above it the allocator gives up and
everything takes a slot. No single test body exercises both.

## Two things found while measuring, neither about allocation

- **kotoba-mir's main could not validate its own backend's output.** The
  fused-multiply and constant-divisor selection kotoba-native emits lived only
  on an unmerged branch. Running kotoba-native's suite against kotoba-mir main
  gave 10 failures and 3 errors; nobody saw it because kotoba-native's pinned
  deps.edn named a commit on that branch, so anything resolving the closure
  got the branch and worked. Merged, clean, both suites green.
- **amu's test suite had not been able to load since that morning.** One
  closing paren too many in a test file; the reader fails before any test runs.
  The suite reports COMPLETE only at 130 of 130 namespaces, which is why the
  difference between *no tests ran* and *all tests passed* was visible at all.

## Meta-review

- **Both prior iterations named the same next step and neither was wrong about
  it.** The convergence was the signal; the cost was visible from two ISAs and
  two workloads.
- **The win was in how the resource is handed out, not how much of it there
  is.** Widening the pool alone would have been a regression on everything
  small. Byte-identical output for what already fit is the evidence that the
  change is additive rather than a redistribution.
- **A check that cannot reach the thing it checks reports success.** The
  callee-saved harness passed on broken code for a real reason — the workload
  never reached that tier. Same shape as iteration 4's benchmark, one layer
  down.
- **Three test failures out of seventeen were the improvement showing up.**
  Five entry arguments no longer spill; a value-position `if` needs no phi
  transports on AArch64. Recorded as what they now are, renamed where the old
  name described the old behaviour.
- **Five more were tests pinning a register when they meant an instruction.**
  They looked for `cmp rcx, <bound>`; the bound check was still there with the
  length in RDX or R8. Matching the instruction rather than the register is
  what they were about — confirmed by changing the bound, which turns all five
  red.

## Next

**The cliff is moved, not removed.** `allocate-without-spills` still fails
whole and `allocate-with-spills` still gives every value a slot; a body needing
twelve live values on x86-64 falls exactly as far as one needing five used to.
Proportional spilling — spill what does not fit, keep the rest in registers —
is the next piece, and unlike everything in iterations 1 through 5 there is now
a workload on each side of the cliff to say whether it worked."}]
